(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has severe congenital pulmonic stenosis with exertional dyspnea and syncope. Which procedure is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Mitral balloon valvotomy","Aortic valve replacement","Pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty","Tricuspid annuloplasty"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe pulmonic stenosis with symptoms is treated with pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty, with diuretics for right heart failure symptoms; severe congenital disease may require valve replacement. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has soft yellow plaques on both upper eyelids near the inner canthi (xanthelasmas). Her low-density lipoprotein is 205 mg/dL. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["They are harmless and need no work-up","They suggest an inherited lipid disorder","They are caused by her eye makeup","They show her triglyceride is very high"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Xanthelasmas usually indicate an underlying genetic condition, and ruling out primary causes matters because they influence her own risk and the management of her family members. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man hospitalized with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction asks why a defibrillator is being discussed. Which cause accounts for about half of cardiac arrests in hospitalized patients like him?","choices":["Acute pulmonary embolism","Low potassium alone","Complete heart block","Ventricular arrhythmias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 52% of cardiac arrests in hospitalized patients with reduced ejection fraction are due to ventricular arrhythmias, which is the reason for defibrillator protection in those who meet the criteria. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has breathlessness on exertion for a week. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 45. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order an implantable device","Look for a non-cardiac cause","Admit for acute heart failure","Start a loop diuretic today"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A level below 100 rules out acute heart failure with a negative predictive value above 90%, so her breathlessness needs another explanation. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 22%) is breathless even at rest. He does not yet take a renin-angiotensin drug. Which renin-angiotensin option fits his symptom class?","choices":["No renin-angiotensin drug once class IV","A calcium channel blocker such as verapamil","A converting enzyme inhibitor or receptor blocker","An angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms at rest are class IV. An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor or receptor blocker covers class II to IV, while the angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor is recommended for class II to III. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had his diuretic dose increased 10 days ago. He now has lightheadedness on standing, a 28 mmHg systolic fall and a heart rate rise from 70 to 94. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Reduce the recently increased diuretic","Start midodrine for the pressure","Start fludrocortisone","Add droxidopa as an adjunct"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The offending agent is removed first: iatrogenic hypotension is treated by reducing or removing the medication, with slow position changes meanwhile. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes and numb feet has a 30 mmHg standing pressure fall with a heart rate rise of only 3 beats and no sign of dehydration. What is the most likely mechanism of her syncope?","choices":["Inadequate norepinephrine release","Reduced volume from fluid loss","A fast rhythm reducing output","A vagal surge with vasodilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Autonomic dysfunction leaves too little norepinephrine released from sympathetic postganglionic neurons, so vessels fail to constrict and the heart rate rises less than expected. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman in stage B, pre-heart failure, developed side effects on lisinopril and could not tolerate it. Which is the recommended alternative?","choices":["Stopping renin-angiotensin therapy","Losartan, an angiotensin receptor blocker","Sacubitril-valsartan combination","Verapamil for blood pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage B lists an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor with an angiotensin receptor blocker if the inhibitor is not tolerated, alongside a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman without diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 140 mg/dL and a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 10%. She is unsure whether to start a statin, and no risk enhancers are present. Which test can help the decision?","choices":["Repeat total cholesterol","Fundoscopic examination","Skin biopsy for receptor activity","Coronary artery calcium score"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In adults 40 to 75 without diabetes, with low-density lipoprotein of 70 to 189 and 10-year risk of 7.5% to 19.9%, coronary artery calcium scoring is considered when the statin decision is uncertain. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What causes mitral valve prolapse?","choices":["Myxomatous degeneration of the valve","Papillary muscle rupture after infarction","Rheumatic scarring of the leaflets","Calcification of an aging valve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse comes from myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve, which leaves thickened, redundant leaflets that flare into the left atrium during systole. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman started a moderate-intensity statin six weeks ago for primary prevention. She feels well. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat the lipid panel now","Stop the statin, she feels well","Recheck lipids in two years","Double the intensity without labs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adherence and percentage response are assessed with a repeat lipid panel 4 to 12 weeks after starting a statin, then every 3 to 12 months as needed; six weeks falls in that window. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man, three days after an acute myocardial infarction, has sudden breathlessness, crackles in both lungs and a new holosystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. Which is the most likely cause?","choices":["Myxomatous degeneration","Rheumatic heart disease","Papillary muscle rupture","Tricuspid annular dilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An acute myocardial infarction with papillary muscle rupture is an acute cause of mitral regurgitation, and acute mitral regurgitation presents with left-sided heart failure symptoms such as dyspnea and rales. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 45, 47"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with exertional chest pressure has a nuclear stress test that is abnormal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Coronary calcium score","Discharge with no follow-up","Repeat the stress test","Cardiac catheterization"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An abnormal nuclear stress test leads to cardiac catheterization, where coronary angiography, the gold standard for diagnosing coronary artery disease, defines the lesions. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 38"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with known dilated cardiomyopathy has worsening breathlessness. Blood pressure is 102/64, a third heart sound (an extra sound in early diastole just after the second heart sound) is present, and he has crackles. Which ejection fraction is the echocardiogram expected to show?","choices":["About 25%","About 60%","About 75%","About 45%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dilated cardiomyopathy is the systolic chain: poor contraction, a reduced ejection fraction of 40% or less, low-normal pressure, a third heart sound and pulmonary edema. A value near 25% fits. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with no known heart disease collapses while jogging and cannot be resuscitated. Autopsy shows extensive coronary artery disease. What is the most likely cause of his sudden cardiac death?","choices":["Electrolyte abnormality","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Complete heart block","Ventricular fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The most common cause of sudden cardiac death is ventricular fibrillation, and sudden death is the initial manifestation of coronary artery disease in over 20% of patients, as it was for him. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has an echocardiogram for palpitations that incidentally shows mild aortic stenosis. He has no chest pain, breathlessness or fainting. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["No follow-up is needed","Start a nitrate for prevention","Cardiology consultation","Transcatheter valve replacement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All patients with evidence of aortic stenosis on echocardiogram should have a cardiology consult to evaluate them and set the frequency of follow-up, even when the finding is mild and incidental. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has just recovered from replacement of his aortic valve with a mechanical valve. Which long-term antithrombotic therapy is recommended?","choices":["Heparin as permanent outpatient therapy","No therapy after the recovery period","Warfarin, a vitamin K antagonist","Aspirin alone as antiplatelet therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In patients with mechanical heart valves, vitamin K antagonist therapy is recommended for long-term management over no vitamin K antagonist and over antiplatelet agents. Warfarin is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 21"},{"q":"A standard troponin I result is 0.12 ng/mL; how is it read?","choices":["Too low to report","Abnormal","Normal for a woman","Not abnormal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL is abnormal, and 0.12 ng/mL is three times that 99th percentile. The value is then repeated to watch the trend. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man starting home monitoring asks how to do it. What should he be told?","choices":["One reading each morning for three days","Two readings morning and evening for seven days","Readings only when he feels unwell","Continuous monitoring over twenty-four hours at home"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the accompanying instruction matters as much: do not overreact to the highest single reading, because acting on outliers generates anxiety and unnecessary changes in therapy. They are taken at least a minute apart and the average is reported. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man in the intensive care unit with acute heart failure has cool extremities and hypotension, and inotropic support is being considered. Which agents are used for this?","choices":["Losartan or irbesartan","Spironolactone or eplerenone","Dobutamine or milrinone","Diltiazem or verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inotropic therapy for intensive care use includes the sympathomimetics dobutamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine and the phosphodiesterase inhibitor milrinone. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which valve replacement is preferred for severe tricuspid stenosis?","choices":["Transcatheter aortic","Human donor","Bioprosthetic","Mechanical"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis needs replacement, a bioprosthetic valve is preferred. Balloon valvotomy and repair are reserved for hepatic congestion or severe systemic venous congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"For a patient at risk for heart failure who has diabetes, which drug class is recommended first?","choices":["Beta blocker as the first agent","Loop diuretic such as furosemide","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In stage A, at risk for heart failure, the one strongly recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes. A beta blocker enters only at stage B, once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman has a pressure of 104/64 at a routine visit and feels well. She worries because it is lower than her usual readings. What should she be told about this reading?","choices":["It proves autonomic nerve failure","Any reading this low needs pressors","Pressure norms are fixed for all ages","One reading is a snapshot in time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each reading is only a snapshot in time and is judged against age, comorbidities and the possibility of relative hypotension, together with symptoms. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man who injects drugs has fevers for two weeks. There is a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border near the epigastrium that grows louder with inspiration (Carvallo sign), and his neck veins pulsate. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tricuspid stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border near the epigastrium that increases with inspiration is tricuspid regurgitation; in an injection drug user the primary cause is infective endocarditis. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 71, 70"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man's blood pressure is 148/88 in the right arm and 132/80 in the left at his first visit. He has no chest pain and his pulses are symmetric and normal. Which arm should be used at follow-up visits?","choices":["Image immediately for aortic dissection","Compare arm and leg pressures to exclude coarctation","Use the right arm for all subsequent measurements","Average the two readings and record 140/84"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both arms are measured at the initial visit precisely to find a difference, and the higher consistent arm becomes the one used for follow-up. Averaging the two would systematically understate his pressure. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which antithrombotic class has fallen out of favor in coronary care?","choices":["Fibrinolytic agents","Cyclooxygenase inhibitors","Glycoprotein 2b/3a inhibitors","P2Y12 receptor antagonists"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glycoprotein 2b/3a inhibitors (eptifibatide, abciximab, tirofiban) have fallen out of favor. Aspirin with a P2Y12 receptor antagonist is the antiplatelet pairing in use. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has supine hypertension (high blood pressure while lying flat). Lying down she is 176/96; after 3 minutes standing she is 152/90, and she feels mildly lightheaded. Do these readings meet the orthostatic criterion?","choices":["Yes; the fall exceeds 20 mmHg","No; readings need 10 minutes","Yes; any fall with symptoms counts","No; a fall over 30 mmHg is needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With an elevated baseline or supine hypertension the systolic fall must exceed 30 mmHg; hers is 24 and the diastolic fall is only 6. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman consistently reads 150/92 in clinic but her seven-day home average is 118/74 with a validated upper-arm device and correct technique. Which pattern does this represent?","choices":["Resistant hypertension","White coat hypertension","Stage 2 hypertension","Masked hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: raised office readings with normal out-of-office readings. Recognizing it avoids treating a patient whose true pressure is normal. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has chest pressure that began at rest. Her electrocardiogram shows T-wave inversion in two consecutive leads. Which result would make this a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Inverted T waves persisting","Troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL","Pain that began at rest","Relief with nitroglycerin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elevated cardiac markers (troponin or high-sensitivity troponin) are what separate a non-ST-elevation infarction from unstable angina. A standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL is abnormal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has leg edema and jugular venous distension (distended neck veins) with a raised B-type natriuretic peptide. Right-sided heart failure is suspected. Which test establishes the definitive diagnosis?","choices":["Repeat natriuretic peptide","Right heart catheterization","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram","Echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clinical heart failure with distended neck veins raises suspicion of right-sided failure, and the echocardiogram gives the definitive diagnosis by ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with new heart failure also describes pressure-like chest pain for the past day. His electrocardiogram shows new T-wave inversions. Which additional laboratory test is indicated?","choices":["Thyroid hormone level","Repeat natriuretic peptide","Hepatic function panel","Cardiac troponin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is ordered when there is concern for an acute infarction or for heart failure secondary to ischemic cardiomyopathy, and chest pain with new inversions raises exactly that concern. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with no cardiac symptoms is found to have pulmonic regurgitation. Which is the most common way this lesion comes to attention?","choices":["Syncope during strenuous exercise","Chest pain with a new infarction","An incidental echo or exam finding","Sudden shock and pulmonary edema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic regurgitation is usually an incidental finding on an echocardiogram or on physical examination; the murmur is soft and hard to hear. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 27%) has blood pressure above goal on her heart failure regimen. Verapamil is proposed. What is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Add it once she reaches class IV","Avoid it and use recommended classes","Use it in place of her beta blocker","Start it as first-line pressure control"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker, and calcium channel blockers are avoided in heart failure. Pressure control comes from optimizing the recommended classes as tolerated. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has chest pain with ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5 and V6. Which artery is the most likely culprit?","choices":["Right coronary artery","Right marginal branch","Posterior interventricular","Left circumflex artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Leads I, aVL, V5 and V6 are the lateral leads, and lateral ST elevation points to the left circumflex artery (or a diagonal branch of the left anterior descending artery). — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 62"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a fasting triglyceride of 780 mg/dL and low-density lipoprotein of 110 mg/dL. Diabetes, kidney disease, alcoholism and hypothyroidism have been excluded. Which management plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Very low-fat diet plus a fibrate","Ezetimibe with a high-carbohydrate diet","Bile acid sequestrant with no diet change","Observation with a recheck in a year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Above 500 mg/dL the plan is a very low-fat diet (under 15% of calories from fat), weight management, physical activity and a fibrate, niacin or both. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has a waist circumference of 38 inches, triglyceride of 190 mg/dL, high-density lipoprotein of 45 mg/dL, blood pressure of 134/88 and fasting glucose of 104 mg/dL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Metabolic syndrome","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Mixed dyslipidemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Any three of five criteria establish it; she meets four: waist above 35 inches, triglyceride above 150, high-density lipoprotein below 50 in a woman and blood pressure above 130/85. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man who drinks heavily is admitted for an unrelated procedure. On the second day his blood pressure rises sharply. What explains this?","choices":["Alcohol withdrawal can produce acute severe hypertension","Only chronic excess raises pressure; withdrawal has no effect","Alcohol lowers pressure, so stopping returns him to baseline","Moderate alcohol should be resumed for cardiovascular benefit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the relationship runs both ways: excess alcohol raises blood pressure chronically, and withdrawal from it can raise it acutely. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has recurrent anginal chest pain during periods of work stress and anxiety. Her electrocardiogram shows ST changes during symptoms, and coronary angiography shows normal coronary arteries. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vasospastic angina","Microvascular angina","Unstable angina","Stable angina"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina (formerly cardiac syndrome X) is angina with ST changes, possibly with elevated biomarkers, but normal coronary arteries on the angiogram; it is more frequent in women and often associated with stress or anxiety. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 93, 94"},{"q":"What is the normal relationship between leg and arm systolic pressure?","choices":["Arm systolic is normally higher than leg","Leg systolic is normally higher than arm","The relationship varies unpredictably and cannot be used","They are normally identical"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so a leg pressure that is not higher is the abnormal finding. Leg pressures are measured when the pulse examination suggests coarctation. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has a faint while urinating at night, with nausea and sweating beforehand and quick recovery. Which test is used to exclude a cardiac dysrhythmia?","choices":["A tilt-table test","A blood glucose level","Orthostatic vital signs","An electrocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A normal electrocardiogram lowers suspicion of a dysrhythmia, just as a normal glucose excludes hypoglycemia and blood tests address infection. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which ejection fraction defines heart failure with preserved ejection fraction?","choices":["65% or more","41% or more","35% or more","50% or more"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Preserved ejection fraction is 50% or more, the same line as a normal ejection fraction. Below that come mildly reduced at 41 to 49% and reduced at 40% or less. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which drug combinations are preferred as initial therapy?","choices":["An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor with an angiotensin receptor blocker","A beta blocker with a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Two thiazide-type diuretics together","A renin-angiotensin blocker with a calcium blocker or diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the classes act by complementary mechanisms. A single-pill combination is preferred where two agents are started, since it improves adherence. That means an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker paired with a dihydropyridine or a thiazide-type diuretic. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"In a patient aged 0 to 19 years, what does a diagnosis of familial hypercholesterolemia call for?","choices":["A statin","Apheresis in every case","A fibrate","Lifestyle alone until age 40"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the 0-to-19 age group, lifestyle prevents or reduces risk, and a diagnosis of familial hypercholesterolemia leads to a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has firm yellow nodules over the backs of his hands, elbows and heels. His total cholesterol is 780 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride, and both parents have markedly elevated cholesterol. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Presentation in childhood with cutaneous xanthomas on the hands, elbows and heels, total cholesterol of about 400 to above 1000 mg/dL, normal triglyceride and two affected parents fits two mutant alleles. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which pattern defines dyslipidemia?","choices":["High cholesterol or triglyceride, or low high-density lipoprotein","Triglyceride elevation only after a fatty meal","Low low-density lipoprotein with high high-density lipoprotein","Any cholesterol value outside a fixed normal range"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dyslipidemia is a disorder of lipoprotein metabolism with elevated total, low-density or non-high-density cholesterol or triglyceride, and/or decreased high-density lipoprotein. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from multiple system atrophy has supine hypertension (high blood pressure while lying flat). He remains symptomatic on standing despite slow position changes. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":["Slow position changes","Midodrine","Fludrocortisone","Droxidopa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Midodrine is best with neurogenic syncope but is avoided with supine hypertension, severe heart disease and chronic kidney disease. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman who smokes a pack of cigarettes a day asks whether smoking really matters for her heart at her age. Which counseling point is accurate?","choices":["It matters only with diabetes","It matters only after menopause","It is a strong predictor at her age","It raises risk only in men"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is a modifiable coronary risk factor and a strong predictor, especially in women younger than 45. Her age makes the counseling more urgent, not less. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man on lisinopril remains above goal. A colleague suggests adding losartan. What is the problem?","choices":["Dual blockade raises hyperkalemia and kidney injury risk","The combination is acceptable if potassium is monitored","The two cancel each other out pharmacologically","Angioedema occurs in most patients on the combination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the instruction is absolute rather than cautionary: one is used INSTEAD of the other. The two look complementary if the mechanism is read loosely, which is why the rule is stated so plainly. It adds no benefit in exchange. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man reads 124/78 in clinic, but his home average over seven days is 142/90. Why does this matter?","choices":["It indicates his home device is miscalibrated","It means he has white coat hypertension","It carries real risk despite normal office readings","It is a benign pattern requiring no follow-up"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is masked hypertension, and it is the reason out-of-office measurement is not merely a way of avoiding overtreatment: it also finds the patients whose risk the clinic reading conceals. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl has palpitations and dizziness on standing. Within 10 minutes of standing her heart rate rises from 70 to 114, and her pressure does not fall. Does this meet the heart rate criterion for her age?","choices":["Yes; the rise exceeds 40 per minute","Yes; any rise over 20 per minute","No; adolescents need a rise of 50","No; the rise must occur in 3 minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In adolescents the threshold is an increase of 40 beats per minute; her rise of 44 within 10 minutes meets it. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with acute severe aortic regurgitation from endocarditis is waiting for valve surgery that is delayed by several hours. Which is the most appropriate temporary stabilization in the intensive care unit?","choices":["Intravenous diuretics and nitroprusside","Oral hydralazine and discharge home","A fluid bolus to raise her preload","Percutaneous balloon valvuloplasty"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If surgery for acute severe aortic regurgitation is delayed, temporary stabilization may be attempted in the intensive care unit with intravenous diuretics and a vasodilator such as nitroprusside. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman presents with acute right hemiparesis and aphasia of two hours' duration. Her blood pressure is 196/104. How should her blood pressure be managed?","choices":["Reduce the pressure to below 130/80 immediately","Follow the acute stroke protocol, not chronic targets","Reduce the mean arterial pressure by a quarter in an hour","Leave the blood pressure entirely untreated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute stroke is specifically excepted from the general emergency reduction approach, because driving the pressure to a chronic target can extend the ischemic injury. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What triggers situational reflex syncope?","choices":["Standing after autonomic nerve damage","Certain bodily functions or actions","A rapid ventricular rhythm","Emotional stress, fear or pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Situational syncope is triggered by certain bodily functions or physical actions, such as urination at night, coughing or sneezing. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How does the heart rate behave in neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["It rises only minimally","It rises significantly","It falls sharply first","It exceeds 120 per minute"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Failed autonomic signaling means the heart rate rises less than expected even as pressure falls, which is the tell that separates it from volume loss. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman on a statin has reached her low-density lipoprotein goal, but her triglyceride remains 260 mg/dL after lifestyle changes. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stop the statin and watch","Begin apheresis sessions","Add a fibrate or niacin","Continue with no change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The primary aim is the low-density goal, and if triglyceride stays above 200 mg/dL once it is reached, additional triglyceride-lowering therapy (a fibrate or niacin) is indicated. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance and palpitations for two months, and now ankle edema and breathlessness. Heart rate is 124 and B-type natriuretic peptide is elevated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Systolic failure from ischemic disease","High-output failure from hyperthyroidism","Right-sided failure from mitral stenosis","Diastolic failure from hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hyperthyroidism raises peripheral demand, and the heart answers with a faster rate and stronger contraction until the heart failure syndrome appears: fatigue, dyspnea, edema and palpitations. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure and congestion is started on furosemide. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["It will raise his potassium to high levels","No blood tests are needed on this drug","Potassium and kidney labs will be checked","His liver enzymes will be checked weekly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Furosemide is a potassium-wasting loop diuretic, so potassium and renal function are followed on the complete metabolic panel. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has confirmed resistant hypertension: adherent, correctly measured, above goal on three complementary agents. What is the first step in management?","choices":["Refer to a hypertension specialist","Stop all agents and restart from the beginning","Add spironolactone immediately","Optimize the diuretic, with chlorthalidone preferred"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it comes before adding anything. An inadequate or inappropriate diuretic is a common reason the other two agents appear to be failing. The diuretic class is matched to renal function. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF and in the right-sided lead V4R, consistent with right ventricular infarction. His blood pressure is 104/66 mmHg. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["A heparin drip","Nitroglycerin","Chewable aspirin","Clopidogrel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a right ventricular infarct, do not give nitroglycerin: it lowers preload, the failing right ventricle loses its Frank-Starling support, output falls further and the patient can go into shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 62, 63"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with heart failure has cool, clammy extremities, a narrow pulse pressure and sleepiness, along with orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and leg edema. Which bedside hemodynamic profile fits?","choices":["Cold and wet","Warm and wet","Cold and dry","Warm and dry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-perfusion signs (cool extremities, narrow pulse pressure, obtundation) plus congestion signs (orthopnea, edema) place her in the cold and wet profile, hypoperfusion with congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
